Git Hooks
*********

.[perex]
A six-line pre-commit hook checks the PHP code style of exactly what goes into the commit, and stays silent while everything is clean. Hooks that fix an AI agent's code after every edit are described on the page [AI Agents |ai-agents].


A Pre-commit Hook
=================

A commit does not take the file on disk; it takes the file's content in the index, that is, what you added to the commit with `git add`. After `git add -p`, which adds only some of the changes of a file, the two differ. So the hook reads the content from the index and passes it in with the `--stdin` option; the `lines` format then prints one line per finding, and nothing when the code is clean:

```shell
#!/bin/sh
status=0
for file in $(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM -- '*.php'); do
	git show ":$file" | dresscode check --stdin "$file" -f lines --skip-excluded || status=1
done
exit $status
```

Save it as `.git/hooks/pre-commit` and make it executable: `chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit`. If you installed DressCode in the project, write `vendor/bin/dresscode` instead of `dresscode` in the script.

When everything is fine, the hook prints nothing and the commit goes through; otherwise it prints each violation on one line with the path, the line, the message and the decision path, and stops the commit:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php:5:5: error: The import of `Gateway` is unused. [imports.unused]
src/Cart.php:15:21: error: A plain string must be written with single quotes. [literals.quotes]
\--

Thanks to `--skip-excluded`, the hook skips a file that the configuration excludes in `excludePaths` or that is outside its `paths`. Without it, that file would be checked too: DressCode checks code from standard input and a file given by name even when the configuration excludes it.

If you want a hook that fixes the code right away, run `fix` over the working tree and add the fixed files to the index. It is convenient, but it means the commit contains changes the developer has not seen. I prefer the first variant: the hook only reports, and I run the fix on purpose.

With the `pre-commit` tool, it is one entry in the configuration. The tool passes file names to DressCode, so here too the command takes `--skip-excluded`:

```yaml
repos:
  - repo: local
    hooks:
      - id: dresscode
        name: DressCode
        entry: dresscode check -f lines --skip-excluded
        language: system
        types: [php]
```

